<header class="detail-head">
	<h2 class="page-hed">Embed a score</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		A standalone custom element that shows one site's Lighthouse scores and
		links to its full report. Drop it on any page — it needs nothing but the
		script and this instance's URL.
	</p>
</header>

<script type="module" src="/js/speedlify2-score.js"></script>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>Live examples</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Hover or tab to a set of scores to see its summary. Each example is a real
		lookup against this instance. At least one emits a generator tag, so its
		summary carries a "Built with" row — most sites here emit none, and the row
		is simply absent for those.
	</p>

	{#
		A deliberate spread, not the top of the leaderboard. Three perfect scores
		in a row demonstrate the green state and nothing else — the component's
		whole job is showing where a site actually sits, so at least one example
		has to be a site with problems.

		Picked by walking the ranked list from both ends, so the set follows the
		corpus rather than needing maintenance. The one pinned URL below is looked
		up in that list rather than assumed, for the same reason.
	#}
	{% set measured = [] %}
	{% for entry in sites.entries %}
		{% if entry.latest %}{% set measured = (measured.push(entry), measured) %}{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{#
		Pinned first, then the spread. A named URL is a liability in a template —
		it rots the moment that site leaves the corpus — so it is looked up rather
		than trusted: no match in `measured`, no row, and the rest still renders.
	#}
	{% set demo = [] %}
	{% set demoUrls = [] %}
	{% for url in ["https://arielsalminen.com/"] %}
		{% for entry in measured %}
			{% if entry.url == url and entry.url not in demoUrls %}
				{% set demo = (demo.push(entry), demo) %}
				{% set demoUrls = (demoUrls.push(entry.url), demoUrls) %}
			{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
	{% endfor %}

	{% set picks = [] %}
	{% if measured.length %}
		{% set picks = (picks.push(measured[0]), picks) %}
		{% if measured.length > 2 %}{% set picks = (picks.push(measured[(measured.length / 2) | round | int]), picks) %}{% endif %}
		{% if measured.length > 1 %}{% set picks = (picks.push(measured[measured.length - 1]), picks) %}{% endif %}
	{% endif %}
	{% for entry in picks %}
		{% if entry.url not in demoUrls %}
			{% set demo = (demo.push(entry), demo) %}
			{% set demoUrls = (demoUrls.push(entry.url), demoUrls) %}
		{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{#
		One more only if the set so far has none: a site that actually emits a
		generator tag. Most of this corpus does not — a static site usually ships
		no generator meta at all — so a spread picked purely by rank can easily be
		all sites where the "Built with" row is absent, which makes the examples
		misrepresent the component.

		Pushing into an array declared outside the loop is the only way to carry a
		value out of one: a `set` inside a loop does not survive it, but the array
		reference does, which is also what makes the length check work as a "first
		match wins" guard.
	#}
	{% set detected = [] %}
	{% for entry in demo %}
		{% if entry.generator %}{% set detected = (detected.push(entry), detected) %}{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}
	{% if not detected.length %}
		{% set withGenerator = [] %}
		{% for entry in measured %}
			{% if entry.generator and not withGenerator.length and entry.url not in demoUrls %}
				{% set withGenerator = (withGenerator.push(entry), withGenerator) %}
			{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
		{% if withGenerator.length %}{% set demo = (demo.push(withGenerator[0]), demo) %}{% endif %}
	{% endif %}

	<div class="embed-demo">
		{% for entry in demo %}
		<div class="embed-row">
			{#
				Prefixed with the plugin's own filter. HtmlBasePlugin rewrites href
				and src, but `speedlify-url` is a custom attribute it knows nothing
				about — left as "/" these would ask the domain root for their data
				and 404 on a project site served from a subdirectory.
			#}
			<speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ '/' | htmlBaseUrl }}" url="{{ entry.url }}"></speedlify2-score>
			<code>{{ entry.displayUrl }}</code>
			{# Only where the page said so itself, rather than presumed from a listing. #}
			{% if entry.generator %}<span class="muted small">Built with {{ entry.generator.name }}</span>{% endif %}
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>Usage</h2>

	<p>The upstream <code>&lt;speedlify-score&gt;</code> on npm is a different component; this one is <code>&lt;speedlify2-score&gt;</code> and is not published there.</p>

	<p>Download the zero dependency JavaScript file manually: <a href="{{ meta.url }}/js/speedlify2-score.js"><code>{{ meta.url }}/js/speedlify2-score.js</code></a></p>

	<div class="notice notice-warn">
		<strong>It would be unwise to hotlink the speedlify2-score component script.</strong>
		<p>
			Third party JavaScript is a big no-no! It also carries no versioning, no deprecation
			policy and no uptime commitment: the file can change shape, move or stop
			being served at any time, and a page embedding it directly would <strong>break with it and with no notice.</strong>
		</p>
	</div>

	<pre><code>&lt;!-- You need to fix this URL to point to your speedlify2-score.js --&gt;
&lt;script type="module" src="speedlify2-score.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;!-- Scores for the page it sits on --&gt;
&lt;speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/"&gt;&lt;/speedlify2-score&gt;

&lt;!-- Or a specific URL --&gt;
&lt;speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/" url="https://example.com/"&gt;&lt;/speedlify2-score&gt;</code></pre>

	<h3>Attributes</h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		Two, and one of them is optional. There is nothing to configure about the
		output: every instance renders the same six circles — the four Lighthouse
		categories, Core Web Vitals, and axe violations — so a page carrying
		several reads as one table rather than a row of different shapes.
		Everything else is one click away in the full report.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid">
		<thead>
			<tr><th class="col-site">Attribute</th><th class="col-site">Effect</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td class="col-site"><code>speedlify-url</code></td><td class="col-site">Required. This instance's URL.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="col-site"><code>url</code></td><td class="col-site">Which site to show. Defaults to the current page.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="finding-data">
	<h2>How it finds the data</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		There is no index to download. The component slugifies the URL in the
		browser with the same rules the generator uses and fetches
		<code>/api/site/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code> directly. One request, under a
		kilobyte, for exactly the site being shown.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		Several components on one page share a single fetch per URL. Normalization
		matches the generator's, so <code>https://Example.com</code> and
		<code>https://example.com/</code> resolve to the same record.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		Working out the filename is string manipulation, not cryptography, so the
		component needs no secure context and runs on a page served over plain
		http. If two sites ever claim one slug, both are published under a hash
		instead and read here as unmeasured — a miss rather than another site's
		numbers.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		<a href="/api/site/{{ demo[0].slug }}.json">See {{ demo[0].displayUrl }}'s data file</a>
	</p>
</section>
